    "media:imagetext": "#10\nfour alcoves off the center - in one of which I am writing near the\nsandbag-barricaded window.\nThe candles threw a flickering light\npartially illuminating the room and revealing the midnight blue\nposts which support a ceiling decorated with gold medalioned filled\nsmall squares against a rich dark red background with cross beams\ncovered with designs of red, green, blue and yellow. The center of\nthe square room was taken up by a table around which we were seated.\nBond did not get back, so I was the only \"barbarian\" present.\nThursdaY.\nArmistice Day - only in history.\nThis noon\ncut loose at the same time as\nyesterday and 15 minutes\nthe drone of many motors was audible but no planes in sight. Mr. Johnson\nMr. Peck and I left the tiffin table and repaired to a little open porch\nnear the roof. Next to Mr. Peck's house, in which I am staying, 1s a\nlarge Chinese grave yard with lots of mounds which provide good shelter\nagainst flying shrapnel. The nearby Chinese Tarmers, ricksha coolles\nand servants all congregate there when an alarm sounds. They come there\nfor the cover afforded by the hundreds of grave mounds and also to be\nnear the American flag which they at least think affords them some\nmeasure of protection.\nThe noise of the motors became louder and\npresently out of the clouds to the south three Japanese twin-motored\ncame into view - below the clouds at about 6000 feet. The sky\nwas about 95 percent overcast at that altitude and only infrequent\nholes showed\na light blue against the gray which of course predominated.\nThe Three bombers came directly at us,\nthen turned away toward the\nairport outside the wall, where no doubt more paper planes had been\nconstructed by those overworked kite-makers. Machine guns tack-tack-\nanti-aircraft guns banged from all quarters and the boom of\nexploding shells overhead kept us under a protruding edge of the roof\nas pieces of shrapnel fell around us.\nIt's wise to keep under some\nprotective covering, for the fragmentation is not always complete and\na spent bit oi shell can give you quite a jolt it it is a large piece\nthat drops on you.\nThe Three bombers in V\nformation dropped their\nbombs on the airport and in the din of rapid explosions we could see\nclouds of dust rising against the horizon some half mile away.\npursuit planes followed and just messed around, one of them stunting\nwhile the other two stayed more or less in formation. One of the\nbombers left formation and heading directly over the city. Mr. Peck\nopined that he was probably going to bomb the City inside the wall,\nbut instead he again flew directly over us, turned and once more\nheaded for the airport, possibly to check the number of \"planes\"\ndestroyed.\nAs he flew over the city wall an anti-aircraft shell burst\nclose to him and we noted a bit of smoke come from the ship just as it\ndisappeared into some clouds. Suddenly we were amazed to hear a lot of\nshouts and hand-clapping\n(a new form of expressing approval among Chinese)\nfrom the grave-yard and looking where they were all excitedly gazing,\nwe saw a streak of black smoke - then a blazing mass which broke into\ntwo brightly burning meteors which continued downward through live thousand\nfeet to hit, still in flames, near the airport.\nThe Japs had traded some\nNippon copy of a Douglas all-metal airplane with crew of six for a few\npaper ones! Great rejoicing in Nanking but I guess it's a different\nstory in six Tokyo homes! However, they died for \"Charley\" and we'll let\nhim worry about that!\nFriday November 12tn.\nAnother rainy day. Trees sticking up into\ncelling. Plan to leave tomorrow by boat from Hankow.\n-10-",
